      "comment": "Confirming 3. pl_002 superseded, its items intact, one active plan left. The rationale credits a plan level rationale that is not in the file: the tool rejected it, and the link survives on the first item as carried forward from pli_006."
    },
    {
      "test_id": "ut_research_plan_010",
      "dimension_source": "rubric",
      "dimension_name": "Jurisdiction accuracy",
      "llm_score": 3,
      "corrected_score": 3,
      "comment": "Confirming 3. Schuylkill County was formed in 1811 and its boundaries were stable across 1860 to 1870, so every county level item sits in a jurisdiction that existed and held those records. Probate is pitched at the right level: Pennsylvania kept wills and estate files with the county Register of Wills, not the state. Civil War service is correctly pitched at Pennsylvania, since compiled service and pension files are organised by state regiment rather than by county. The plan also does not reach for a state death record, which is right. Pennsylvania had no statewide death registration until 1906, so for a death around 1865 the county and the church registers are the only places to look."
    }
